desc:Mark 73 [tine e-piano] (GFX)
//tags: instrument electric piano tine stage suitcase tremolo physical-modeling
//author: JGWizrad / Garden FX (GFX)
//
// Requires gfx-drift, gfx-output, gfx-ui, gfx-panel, gfx-instrument, gfx-voice,
// gfx-dyn and gfx-faceplate (.jsfx-inc) in the same folder.
//
// MARK 73. A sample-free tine electric piano in the spirit of the classic 70s stage and suitcase tine pianos,
// built from the mechanism up rather than from envelopes and filters:
//
// HAMMER   a nonlinear neoprene / felt contact (force = K x compression^p, with Hunt-Crossley loss). The hammer
//          flies at the key velocity, compresses against the tine and bounces off: harder hits compress further,
//          stay in contact for less time and so excite more of the high tine partials (HARDNESS sets K and p).
// TINE     each key's tine is a clamped-free cantilever with a tuning spring: five modal resonators at its
//          inharmonic ratios (1 : ~6.8 : ~18 : ~35 : ~58, the second one, the "bell", pulled to sit musically),
//          each with its own decay (the high partials die in milliseconds, the fundamental sings). The hammer
//          strikes near the clamp, so its force reaches each mode by that mode's shape at the strike point.
// TONEBAR  the heavy arm of the asymmetric tuning fork: a resonator tuned a fraction of a hertz off the tine,
//          lightly coupled to it. A little energy flows tine -> tonebar -> tine, so the tine's swing (and with it
//          the pickup's harmonics) swells and eases slowly: an amplitude / timbre cycle, not a pitch wobble.
// PICKUP   the tine TIP DISPLACEMENT is read by a magnetic pickup: the flux is a bell-shaped function of the tip's
//          position across the pole, the voltage is d(flux)/dt. VOICING is the tech's voicing screw: left = DARK, the
//          tine far off the pole axis (fundamental, nearly a sine, a little low-order colour); centre = the classic
//          factory voicing; right = DYNO, the tine inline with the pole (the curve is symmetric, so the output is the
//          octave: shallow and bright). GAP = how far the pole sits from the swing. Slammed notes swing past the pole
//          and fold the curve: the bark and growl. As the note decays the swing shrinks and the harmonic balance keeps
//          moving. The pickup runs at 2x (exact half-sample tine positions) and is decimated, so it does not alias.
// TAME     a dynamic top-end control: above ~2.8 kHz (Linkwitz-Riley split) an envelope-following gain turns the
//          band down as it rises; its strength follows HARDNESS, BELL and VOICING (zero for soft, mellow settings),
//          so hard, bright, bell-heavy or dyno playing stays warm instead of clanging.
// LAYERS   tine fundamental, octave (pickup position), BELL (the bell / chime partial, its own level), hammer clink
//          (the short bright high modes, heard on hard hits), thump, growl (over-swing), damper and key-off noise
//          (MECH sets the noises). The bell rings with a gentle velocity curve and reaches the output mostly through the
//          pickup's straight response (no ring-mod sidebands); the pitch does not follow the dynamics.
// KEYS     each key has its own fixed (seeded, repeatable) personality: tuning (+/-0.15 cent), tonebar beat (a faint
//          amplitude swell, 0.3 - 1 Hz, never a pitch wobble), pickup alignment, partial balance and decay.
// RELEASE  the damper felt lands on the tine (fast but not instant, a soft damper tick); key-off knock; sustain
//          pedal (CC64) with a little sympathetic ring between the undamped tines.
// AMP      three stages: (a) tone stack (STAGE flat; SUITCASE bass and treble shelving with a mid scoop), (b) the
//          valves / transistors, tanh(x g + b) - tanh(b), driven by OVERDRIVE (the bias adds even harmonics),
//          (c) speaker (STAGE: direct, rolled off ~10 kHz; SUITCASE: 4 x 12 open cabinet, ~5 kHz low-pass, mid bump,
//          ~100 Hz resonance), then TREBLE. MODULATION (right: tremolo, left: stereo auto-pan) at RATE runs through
//          incandescent bulbs and photocells (the filament heats faster than it cools): smooth and rounded.
// PLAYING  VELOCITY (0 % = one fixed hammer speed, 100 % = a long hammer throw: the widest soft-to-slammed range),
//          WIDTH (keys spread low-left to high-right), POLYPHONY, FINE, RANDOM (a small random offset on every note).
// DRIFT    GFX instrument drift, kept gentle (15 % here): the tuning wanders well under a cent, every note lands
//          a fraction of a cent off, the coil's top end and the pickup alignment breathe, the modulation rate wavers.
// FX       after the amp, as in Harbour Keys (after GFX Mod Rack): CHORUS (stereo BBD-style: RATE, DEPTH), VIBE
//          (lamp-driven photocell phaser: RATE, DEPTH), DELAY (ping-pong with warm saturating feedback: TIME in ms or
//          synced to the tempo, FEEDBACK, MIX), SPACE (ROOM / PLATE / GATED, AMOUNT); each has its own lit ON key.
// STRIP    an EDIT key in the header opens a full channel strip page (the Wideload rack: AR-70 / SX-6 pre,
//          VX-4 / MT-7 EQ, GB-2 / VM-67 compressor, ORDER, A/B models, meters). Every unit starts switched out;
//          FX PRE / POST puts the strip before or after the FX (swapped under a 4 ms dip, never a click).
// MIDI     note on / off, sustain (CC64), all notes off (CC120 / CC123).

import gfx-drift.jsfx-inc
import gfx-output.jsfx-inc
import gfx-ui.jsfx-inc
import gfx-panel.jsfx-inc
import gfx-instrument.jsfx-inc
import gfx-voice.jsfx-inc
import gfx-dyn.jsfx-inc
import gfx-faceplate.jsfx-inc

slider1:50<0,100,1>-Decay (%)
slider2:40<0,100,1>-Release (%)
slider3:50<0,100,1>-Hardness (%)
slider4:50<0,100,1>-Voicing (dark - dyno, %)
slider5:50<0,100,1>-Pickup gap (%)
slider6:0<-12,12,0.1>-Treble (dB)
slider7:0<-100,100,1>-Modulation (pan - / trem +, %)
slider8:4.5<0.1,12,0.01>-Rate (Hz)
slider9:70<0,100,1>-Velocity (%)
slider10:30<0,100,1>-Width (%)
slider11:16<4,32,1>-Polyphony
slider12:0<-50,50,0.1>-Fine (cents)
slider13:0<0,10,0.1>-Random (cents)
